## Tests

The same image, one suite at a time under the lock:

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
      fpq-integration tests.sh'
    -> extension: make installcheck: All 1 tests passed.
       client: Ran 106 tests ... OK (skipped=2: the opt-in eventfd tests)

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
      -e FASTPOSTGRES_TRANSPORT=eventfd fpq-integration tests.sh'
    -> extension: make installcheck: All 1 tests passed.
       client: Ran 106 tests ... OK

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
      -e FASTPOSTGRES_MEMFD=1 fpq-integration tests.sh'
    -> extension: make installcheck: All 1 tests passed.
       client: Ran 106 tests ... OK (skipped=2: the opt-in eventfd tests)

All three suites are fully green. The memfd run includes the sealed-memfd tests
and the two assertions that failed in the earlier `merge/read-path` record: that
branch's store is now the default and the tests assert the memfd behavior rather
than `/dev/shm` filenames.

### AB

100K, baseline -> integration (rows/s):

    DBAPI                 3.67M -> 11.58M   +215.5%
    stream()              9.20M -> 14.29M    +55.3%   (zero-copy section)
    stream() (paths)      9.74M -> 15.07M    +54.7%
    core interface        3.66M -> 11.23M   +206.8%
    4 processes           8.86M -> 33.30M   +275.8%
    adbc arrow table      2.31M ->  2.32M     +0.4%
    stream() vs Arrow     3.97x ->  6.30x   (9.20M/2.32M -> 14.29M/2.27M)

1M, baseline -> integration (rows/s):

    DBAPI                 4.00M -> 13.87M   +246.8%
    stream()              9.23M -> 13.30M    +44.1%   (zero-copy section)
    stream() (paths)      9.39M -> 14.52M    +54.6%
    core interface        3.98M -> 13.84M   +247.7%
    4 processes           9.57M -> 49.78M   +420.2%
    adbc arrow table      2.36M ->  2.33M     -1.3%
    stream() vs Arrow     4.09x ->  5.75x   (9.23M/2.26M -> 13.30M/2.31M)

The DBAPI/core jump is the default read path: the v3 streaming receiver (H8-B)
plus the lazy `ResultSet`/block decode, the safe object pool and the reduced
per-turn worker cost. `stream()` is the zero-copy transport itself; the v3 frame
adds the column offset table and removes the full-batch compaction, which is
where its 100K/1M gain comes from. The Arrow rival is unchanged within noise, so
the `stream()`-vs-Arrow ratio moves from ~4.0x to 5.75-6.30x (575-630% of Arrow).

**One honest combined read multiple:** the default DBAPI read at 1M rows,
`4.00M -> 13.87M rows/s` (**3.47x**); at 100K it is `3.67M -> 11.58M` (**3.16x**).
The zero-copy `stream()` read is 1.44x/1.55x over baseline and 5.75x/6.30x Arrow.
No workstream's factor is multiplied into another's; the cells above are the raw
medians from the two images.

This is the whole tree against the protocol baseline (`main`), so it is cumulative
over both merge phases, not only the nine backlog branches. For context, the
previous record (`team-merge-ab.log`) measured `merge/read-path` alone at
`3.95M -> 9.03M` (2.29x) on its own run; the 1M DBAPI cell here is 13.87M. The
two records are separate runs, so read the 3.47x as the full-tree result and the
2.29x only as where the earlier base stood.
